<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Esino</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aesis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Esino is a river of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, about 90 km long, flowing into the Adriatic near Falconara. In antiquity, as the Aesis, it marked the southern limit of the land settled by the Senones and later a boundary of Roman Italy. The town of Jesi stands on its banks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/esino.jpg" alt="Esino" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JollyPR · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Esino_a_Monte_Roberto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
